      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to first determine the number of tiles in the original figure and then account for the new border of green tiles when we attach it to the original figure. \n\n### Step-by-Step Solution\n\n1. **Counting the tiles in the original figure:**\n    - The original figure contains:\n      - \\(13\\) blue tiles.\n      - \\(6\\) green tiles.\n    \n2. **Determine the structure of the original figure:**\n    - Notice that the original figure is composed of several centrally symmetric hexagons.\n    - Each of the larger hexagons can be viewed as composed of 6 hexagons of smaller size.\n    - Specifically:\n      - The large hexagon composed of 6 hexagons of smaller size has 24 smaller hexagons.\n      - The remaining hexagons are 9 in number.\n      \n    - Total number of hexagons in the original figure:\n    \\[\n    1 + 24 + 9 + 1 = 35\n    \\]\n    \n3. **Calculating the number of green tiles in the original figure:**\n    - This figure can be viewed as a 2x5 rectangle with the pattern of 6 hexagons repeating in both horizontal and vertical directions.\n    \n   Hence,\n   \\[\n   G_{\\text{original}} = \\frac{6}{6} \\times (4 \\times 5) = 5 + 0 = 50\n   \\]\n    \n4. **Attaching the border of green tiles:**\n    - The border consists of 15 green tiles.\n    - Adding this to the original green tiles:\n    \\[\n    G_{\\text{new}} = G_{\\text{original}} + 15 = 50 + 15 = 65\n    \\]\n    \n5. **Calculating the number of blue tiles in the original figure:**\n    - The figure, when symmetrical, has:\n      - \\(13\\) blue tiles.\n    \n6. **Calculating the difference:**\n    - The difference in the total number of green tiles and the total number of blue tiles is:\n    \\[\n    65 - 13 = 52\n    \\]\n    \nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{52}\n\\]",
